      "An IQ of 64 is near the upper boundary of the mild intellectual disability range, just six points below the borderline threshold of 70. Cognitive abilities at this level are notably functional across most everyday demands. Reading at a third- to fourth-grade level is achievable, with phonics skills established and comprehension of simple paragraphs solid. Written arithmetic at a second- to third-grade level is accessible; calculator-assisted arithmetic extends further. Working memory supports sentences of moderate complexity and multi-step instructions of three to four steps. Individuals at this level often navigate their community with real independence — using public transit, managing banking with debit cards, shopping from lists. The primary areas of challenge involve abstract reasoning, inferential comprehension, and complex planning across longer time horizons.",
    researchContext:
      "NLSY79 data analyzed across multiple studies do not reliably distinguish outcomes at IQ 64 versus IQ 70 in occupational domains when job supports are available. Research by Cimera (2012) shows that supported employment costs decrease over time as individuals develop job mastery, and that long-term employment outcomes for individuals with IQs in the 60s are similar to those for individuals with IQs in the 70s when job match quality is controlled. The most robust predictor of employment success in this range is the quality of the initial job placement and the employer relationship, not the measured IQ.",
    realWorldScenarios:
      "A 27-year-old with an IQ of 64 works full-time at a commercial laundry facility — sorting, washing, drying, and folding institutional linens on a production schedule. They meet productivity benchmarks consistently and have been promoted to operate a flatwork ironing machine, a task requiring sustained attention and physical coordination. They commute independently by bus. Outside work, they take an online adaptive cooking course, completing modules at their own pace with repeat viewings. They are actively planning for their own apartment, having saved steadily for eight months — a goal they set with their support coordinator and are executing largely independently.",
