      "Gottfredson (1997) identified IQ 70 as a threshold at which military service entry requirements have historically been set, reflecting research on trainability for complex occupational tasks. However, her review also noted that many jobs at lower complexity levels are performed well by individuals at and below this threshold. Schmidt and Hunter (1998) showed that cognitive ability predicts job performance across the full IQ range but that the predictive increment at lower IQ levels applies mainly to training speed, not ultimate performance in well-matched roles. The NLSY79 includes respondents at this IQ level across a wide range of occupational and life outcomes.",
    realWorldScenarios:
      "A 28-year-old with an IQ of 70 works as a cashier at a pharmacy, processing transactions, managing the register, and answering basic customer questions. They have been in the role for two years and recently trained two new employees on the register system. They hold a driver's license and commute independently. At home, they manage an apartment, cook from simple recipes, budget monthly expenses using a spreadsheet template set up initially with help, and are saving toward a used car upgrade. They participate in a community recreation league and are regarded by teammates as a reliable, valued member of the group.",
