      "Research using the NLSY79 by Murray (1998) and others shows that individuals with IQs in the 75–85 range — the borderline to low average zone — are the most common occupants of skilled working-class and lower service-sector jobs in the U.S. economy. These are real, valued, well-compensated roles. The research shows that in this IQ range, non-cognitive traits — conscientiousness, reliability, social skills, and perseverance — predict occupational outcomes as strongly as IQ, and that individuals who develop these traits achieve career trajectories matching those of higher-IQ individuals in comparable roles.",
