      "Schmidt and Hunter's (1998) meta-analysis found that for medium-complexity jobs — which constitute the majority of the U.S. employment market — cognitive ability explains roughly 25% of variance in job performance ratings, leaving 75% to be explained by other factors. At IQ 84, the individual falls within the low end of the medium-ability zone for most occupational categories, meaning that non-cognitive factors — motivation, experience, work ethic, interpersonal skill — are as important as IQ in determining career outcomes. NLSY79 data confirm that income at IQ 84 spans an enormous range depending on these non-cognitive factors.",
